Knowledge and Skills

If an actor has skills or knowledge that exceed those possessed by most others, these skills or knowledge are circumstances to be taken into account in determining whether the actor has behaved as a reasonably careful person.

This rule and its predecessor in the Second Restatement § 289(b) has been applied in many cases. Two recent examples are Dakter v. Cavallino, 358 Wis.2d 434, 856 N.W.2d 523 (Wis.App. 2014) (semi-truck driver required to exercise the degree of care that a reasonable semi-truck driver would exercise under similar circumstances) and Oakey, Estate of Lucero v. May Maple Pharmacy, Inc., 399 P.3d 939 (N.M.App. 2017) (liability of pharmacy that filled physician-prescribed medications including opioids leading to patient’s death due to an overdose). How would you apply this rule if the one with the expertise is the plaintiff? See Jackson v. Axelrad, 221 S.W.3d 650 (Tex. 2007).

It is important to understand that special knowledge and skill is one of the circumstances to consider when assessing negligence. It is not the case that people with special skill are held to a higher legal standard than that of a reasonable person under the circumstances. See Evans v. Hood Corp., 211 Cal.Rptr.3d 261 (Cal.App. 2016).
